And their buying style?

Both Gen Z and younger Millennials are research-heavy, value-conscious digital buyers who rely heavily on peer proof, though younger Millennials prioritize proven ROI and convenience while Gen Z prioritizes authenticity, low-friction mobile checkout, and immediate gratification.
When selling a soft life system to these two cohorts, how you position, price, and structure your checkout is just as important as the product itself.

Predominant Buying Styles


Younger Millennials (Ages 30–37)

• Value- and ROI-Driven. Having navigated major economic shifts, they are willing to invest in higher-ticket solutions if they clearly see how it saves them time, reduces burnout, or improves their financial stability.
• Thorough Researchers. They read reviews, compare alternatives, and often check a creator's long-form content (podcasts, YouTube, or newsletters) to build trust before buying.
• Convenience-Focused. They favor frictionless digital delivery, clear refund policies, and structured bundles that solve a complete problem in one purchase rather than piecing tools together.

Generation Z (Ages 14–29)

• Skeptical of Traditional Ads, Driven by Peer Proof. Gen Z actively ignores overly polished sales pages and relies on unscripted user-generated content (UGC), comment sections, and creator transparency.
• Impulse-Friendly on Low-Ticket Offers. While cautious with large purchases, they readily buy low-ticket digital products ($7–$37) directly through social media links if the aesthetic and immediate utility resonate.
• Values-Aligned. They buy from brands and creators whose personal values, mental health advocacy, and lifestyle align with their own identity.

